#slide[Two ways to draw 3D][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    [
      #align(center, canvas(13, 8.2, {
        // rasterisation: a triangle projected onto the screen
        seg((6, 0.6), (6, 7.4), col: dim, w: 2pt)
        label((5.1, 7.5), [screen], col: dim)
        place(polygon(fill: accent.transparentize(55%), stroke: 1.5pt + accent, (9.5cm, 1.6cm), (12.4cm, 3.6cm), (10.3cm, 6.4cm)))
        dot((1, 4), col: fg)
        label((0.4, 4.4), [eye], col: dim)
        for p in ((9.5, 1.6), (12.4, 3.6), (10.3, 6.4)) { seg((1, 4), p, col: accent.transparentize(30%), w: 1pt, dash: "dashed") }
        seg((6, 2.33), (6, 4.86), col: glow, w: 4pt)
      }))
      #cap[*Rasterising*: project each triangle's corners onto the screen, fill the pixels between. Shapes must be meshes.]
    ],
    [
      #align(center, canvas(13, 8.2, {
        // ray marching: a ray per pixel, walking until it hits
        seg((4, 0.6), (4, 7.4), col: dim, w: 2pt)
        label((3.1, 7.5), [screen], col: dim)
        ring((10.2, 4.3), 1.8, col: accent, w: 2pt, fill: accent.transparentize(70%))
        dot((1, 4), col: fg)
        label((0.4, 4.4), [eye], col: dim)
        for y in (2.2, 3.1, 4.0, 4.9, 5.8) {
          arrow((1, 4), (12.6, 4 + (y - 4) * 3.9), col: glow.transparentize(40%), w: 1pt)
          dot((4, y), col: glow, r: 2.5pt)
        }
      }))
      #cap[*Ray marching*: for each pixel, follow a ray from the eye and ask the scene "how far to the nearest surface?" until it is ≈ 0.]
    ])
]

#slide[Shapes as distance functions][
  #grid(columns: (1.35fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    fig("field-shapes.png", [The distance field of a circle and a box: orange outside, blue inside, a band every 0.1, the surface white], width: 100%),
    [
      A *signed distance function* $d(p)$ gives the distance from $p$ to the nearest surface (negative inside).
      ```
      def sd_circle p r = length p - r

      def sd_box p b =
        let q = abs p - b
        in length (max q 0)
           + min (max q.x q.y) 0

      def scene p =
        min (sd_circle (p - c1) 0.45)
            (sd_box (p - c2) (0.35, 0.3))
      ```
      A scene is the *minimum* of its parts.
    ])
]

#slide[Sphere tracing][
  #grid(columns: (1.4fr, 1fr), gutter: 0.8cm,
    align(center, canvas(15, 8.5, {
      // circles computed from the scene's distance function (a sphere and a
      // wall): each step's circle just touches the nearest surface
      let pts = ((0.6, 5.4, 4.63), (5.14, 4.53, 0.79), (5.91, 4.38, 0.48), (6.39, 4.29, 0.42), (6.8, 4.21, 0.47), (7.26, 4.12, 0.61), (7.87, 4.0, 0.93), (8.78, 3.83, 1.57), (10.32, 3.53, 2.89), (13.15, 2.99, 0.45), (13.59, 2.9, 0.01))
      ring((6.0, 2.3), 1.6, col: accent, w: 2pt, fill: accent.transparentize(70%))
      place(dx: 13.6cm, rect(width: 2cm, height: 8.5cm, fill: accent.transparentize(70%), stroke: none))
      seg((13.6, 0), (13.6, 8.5), col: accent, w: 2pt)
      seg((0.6, 5.4), (13.59, 2.9), col: dim, w: 1pt, dash: "dotted")
      for (x, y, r) in pts { ring((x, y), r, col: glow.transparentize(45%), w: 1pt); dot((x, y)) }
      label((0.1, 5.8), [camera], col: dim)
      label((4.9, 0.1), [sphere], col: accent)
      label((12.2, 7.6), [wall], col: accent)
    })),
    [
      $d(p)$ is a *safe step*: nothing is nearer, so the ray can jump that far without passing through anything.
      ```
      loop t = 0 while t < tmax do
        let d = scene (ro + t * rd)
        in if d < eps * t then hit t
           else t + d
      ```
      Big steps in the open; small ones skimming the sphere; then it speeds up again.
    ])
]

#slide[Combining shapes is arithmetic][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr, 1fr), gutter: 0.6cm,
    fig("field-min.png", [`min a b`: union], width: 100%),
    fig("field-smin.png", [`smin k a b`: smooth union, a fillet of size k], width: 100%),
    fig("field-subtract.png", [`max a (-b)`: subtract b], width: 100%))
  #v(0.4em)
  #grid(columns: (1.2fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    ```
    def smin k a b =
      let h = clamp 0 1 (0.5 + 0.5 * (b - a) / k)
      in mix b a h - k * h * (1 - h)
    ```,
    [The robe flows into the shoulders, the arms into the robe, the cradle's prongs into its collar: all `smin`.])
]

#slide[Transform the point, not the shape][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    [
      #fig("field-repeat.png", [*Repetition*: fold space into cells (`p - cell * round (p / cell)`), and one circle becomes infinitely many], width: 100%)
      #note[The rune circle and the orb's prongs are folded around an axis the same way.]
    ],
    [
      #fig("field-bend.png", [*Bending*: rotate each point by an angle growing with its height, then ask an ordinary capsule], width: 100%)
      #note[The hat droops this way. Deforming space stretches distances, so they are scaled down by the worst stretch to keep steps safe.]
    ])
]

#slide[What each pixel knows][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr, 1fr, 1fr), gutter: 0.4cm,
    fig("shaded.png", [final colour], width: 100%),
    fig("normals.png", [surface normal (as RGB)], width: 100%),
    fig("albedo.png", [material colour], width: 100%),
    fig("ao.png", [ambient occlusion], width: 100%))
  #v(0.5em)
  The *normal* is the gradient of $d$: four samples on a tetrahedron around the hit point. *Albedo* comes from the hit part's material and its procedural texture. *AO* asks $d$ how crowded the surroundings are. Lighting combines them per light.
  #note[These are the drawer's "View" modes, for debugging.]
]

#slide[Shadows and occlusion from the same function][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 1.2cm,
    [
      #align(center, canvas(13.5, 7, {
        seg((0, 6.2), (13.5, 6.2), col: dim, w: 2pt)
        ring((6.5, 3.3), 1.2, col: accent, w: 2pt, fill: accent.transparentize(70%))
        dot((1.5, 6.2), col: fg)
        arrow((1.5, 6.2), (12.8, 0.6), col: glow, w: 1.3pt)
        label((11.6, 0.0), [to light], col: glow)
        // closest approach of the shadow ray to the occluder
        seg((6.5, 3.3), (7.04, 4.38), col: warm, w: 1.3pt, dash: "dashed")
        label((7.2, 4.4), [$d$ at distance $t$], col: warm)
        label((0.4, 6.4), [surface point], col: dim)
      }))
      #cap[*Soft shadow*: march towards the light. The smallest $k · d \/ t$ along the way says how close the ray grazed something: 0 is full shadow, near misses give a penumbra.]
    ],
    [
      #align(center, canvas(13.5, 7, {
        // a corner: floor and wall
        place(dx: 0cm, dy: 6.2cm, rect(width: 6cm, height: 0.8cm, fill: panel, stroke: none))
        place(dx: 6cm, dy: 0cm, rect(width: 7.5cm, height: 7cm, fill: panel, stroke: none))
        seg((0, 6.2), (6, 6.2), col: dim, w: 2pt)
        seg((6, 6.2), (6, 0), col: dim, w: 2pt)
        let x = 4.4
        arrow((x, 6.2), (x, 1.3), col: fg, w: 1pt)
        label((2.4, 0.9), [normal], col: dim)
        for h in (0.6, 1.4, 2.4, 3.6) {
          let q = (x, 6.2 - h)
          ring(q, calc.min(6 - x, h), col: glow.transparentize(55%), w: 1pt)
          dot(q, col: glow)
        }
        label((6.4, 2.7), [wall], col: dim)
      }))
      #cap[*Ambient occlusion*: step out along the normal. Where $d$ at a step is less than the distance stepped (circles cut off by the wall), the corner gets less sky light.]
    ])
]

#slide[Textures without images][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1.25fr), gutter: 0.8cm,
    fig("head.png", [stars, band, brows, eyes and beard locks: hashes, noise and SDFs], width: 100%),
    [
      #fig("cottage.png", [bricks: cell coordinates with every other row offset, a hash per brick, the mortar as a *bump*], width: 100%)
      #v(0.3em)
      *Bump mapping* tilts the normal by a pattern's gradient. *Footprint filtering*: a pixel at distance $t$ is $≈ 0.0008 t$ wide (at 1080p); patterns finer than that fade out instead of flickering.
    ])
]

#slide[Glass, glow and media][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr, 1fr), gutter: 0.6cm,
    fig("orb-lightning.png", [lightning: distance to jagged polylines gives a hot core and a halo], width: 100%),
    fig("orb-fire.png", [fire: rising noise inside, embers drifting up], width: 100%),
    fig("orb-wind.png", [wind: a vortex, and wisps on a tilted ring], width: 100%))
  #v(0.3em)
  The *orb* is solved exactly (ray–sphere) and the ray *refracted* in and out with a *Fresnel* reflection. *Glows* are *media*: each march step adds light $∝$ a density such as $e^(-d \/ "extent")$; steps are capped near them so none is skipped.
]

#slide[Terrain: a heightfield with care][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 0.6cm,
    fig("terrain.png", [noise octaves, ridges, carved rivers, a sea plane], width: 100%),
    fig("terrain-normals.png", [its normals, from the *analytic gradient*: the noise is differentiated alongside its value, one evaluation instead of four], width: 100%))
  #v(0.2em)
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1.1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    align(center, canvas(13, 3.6, {
      // a heightfield and the bound: height above it, scaled by the slope
      let h(x) = 2.4 + 0.7 * calc.sin(x * 0.9) + 0.35 * calc.sin(x * 2.3)
      let hs = range(0, 27).map(i => (i * 0.5, h(i * 0.5)))
      for i in range(hs.len() - 1) { seg(hs.at(i), hs.at(i + 1), col: accent, w: 2pt) }
      dot((6.5, 0.4))
      seg((6.5, 0.4), (6.5, h(6.5)), col: glow, w: 1.2pt, dash: "dashed")
      label((6.8, 0.6), [$y - h(x)$], col: glow)
    })),
    [Height above the ground is not a distance on slopes, but the slope is bounded, so $0.6 (y - h)$ is a safe step. Far above, a *cheaper bound* (first octaves only) stands in.])
]

#slide[Infinite buildings from a hashed grid][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1.1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    [
      #align(center, canvas(13, 9, {
        for i in range(0, 5) { seg((i * 3 + 0.5, 0), (i * 3 + 0.5, 9), col: dim, w: 0.8pt) }
        for j in range(0, 4) { seg((0, j * 3), (13, j * 3), col: dim, w: 0.8pt) }
        // a building in some cells, kept a quarter cell inside its cell
        for (cx, cy, ox, oy) in ((1, 1, 1.4, 0.9), (2, 0, 0.8, 1.6), (3, 2, 1.7, 1.2), (0, 2, 1.0, 0.8)) {
          place(dx: (cx * 3 + 0.5 + ox) * 1cm, dy: (cy * 3 + oy) * 1cm, rect(width: 0.7cm, height: 0.7cm, fill: accent.transparentize(30%), stroke: 1.2pt + accent))
        }
        let p = (8.2, 4.3)
        dot(p)
        seg(p, (9.5, 4.3), col: warm, w: 1.3pt, dash: "dashed")
        label((7.6, 4.6), [to the border], col: warm, size: 12pt)
        ring(p, 1.3, col: glow.transparentize(30%), w: 1.2pt)
      }))
      #cap[In a 30-unit grid, a hash of the cell decides: a building or not, which, where, how turned.]
    ],
    [
      Marching every cell would be ruinous. Every shortcut returns a *lower bound* on the true distance:
      - other cells' buildings stay inside their cells, so they are at least as far as this cell's border (the circle)
      - above every roof: the height above the tallest
      - the march re-checks buildings only when an earlier bound, less the distance travelled since, could be the nearest
      #v(0.3em)
      #note[Cottages, towers, pyramids: a few SDF primitives each.]
    ])
]

#slide[Two marches per ray][
  #grid(columns: (1.3fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    align(center, canvas(15, 8.5, {
      ring((4.5, 4), 2.6, col: glow.transparentize(30%), w: 1.5pt, fill: glow.transparentize(90%))
      label((2.6, 0.9), [local sphere], col: glow)
      place(dx: 3.95cm, dy: 3.1cm, rect(width: 1.1cm, height: 2.2cm, radius: 4pt, fill: rgb("#8a3fc0")))
      let h(x) = 7.3 + 0.4 * calc.sin(x * 0.8) + 0.2 * calc.sin(x * 2.1)
      let hs = range(0, 31).map(i => (i * 0.5, h(i * 0.5)))
      for i in range(hs.len() - 1) { seg(hs.at(i), hs.at(i + 1), col: accent, w: 2pt) }
      dot((0.3, 2.2), col: fg)
      // the land march runs the whole ray; the local one only inside the sphere
      seg((0.3, 2.2), (13.8, 7.0), col: accent.transparentize(30%), w: 2.5pt)
      seg((2.05, 2.82), (6.95, 4.57), col: glow, w: 4pt)
      dot((13.8, 7.0), col: accent)
      label((8.6, 4.3), [land march: the whole ray], col: accent)
      label((4.4, 5.9), [local march: inside the sphere], col: glow)
    })),
    [
      Millimetre fingers next to a 150-unit horizon: one march over everything would take tiny steps everywhere.
      #v(0.3em)
      So the *land* (terrain, water, buildings) and the *local scene* (wizard, rock, glows) are marched separately, each with its own bounds, and the nearer hit wins.
      #v(0.3em)
      #note[Every part has a *layer* bit, so one scene function serves the camera, shadows (only some parts cast) and debug toggles.]
    ])
]

#slide[Over-relaxed sphere tracing][
  #grid(columns: (1.3fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    align(center, canvas(15, 7, {
      seg((0, 5.6), (15, 5.6), col: accent, w: 2pt)
      label((12.8, 5.9), [ground], col: accent)
      for x in (0.5, 2.3, 4.1, 5.8, 7.3, 8.5) { dot((x, 1.2), col: dim, r: 3pt) }
      seg((0.5, 1.2), (8.5, 1.2), col: dim.transparentize(50%), w: 1pt)
      label((9.2, 0.95), [safe steps: 6 to here], col: dim)
      let y2 = 3.3
      for x in (0.5, 3.0, 5.4, 7.7, 9.6) { dot((x, y2), col: glow) }
      seg((0.5, y2), (9.6, y2), col: glow.transparentize(40%), w: 1pt)
      arrow((9.6, y2), (11.4, y2), col: warm, w: 1.3pt)
      arrow((11.4, y2 + 0.45), (10.2, y2 + 0.45), col: warm, w: 1.3pt)
      label((9.8, 4.1), [overshoot: back off], col: warm, size: 13pt)
      label((0.4, 2.55), [steps of 1.4 × d: 4 to the same point], col: glow)
    })),
    [
      Take steps *1.4×* the safe distance. If the next point's bound shows the step jumped past what the last one allowed (the two spheres don't overlap), step back and continue normally.
      #v(0.3em)
      Fewer steps on long, grazing terrain rays.
      #note[Keinert et al., "Enhanced Sphere Tracing", 2014. 1.3 and 1.6 measured slower than 1.4 here.]
    ])
]

#slide[Futhark][
  #grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 1cm,
    [
      A pure, statically typed, array language (think a small ML) that compiles `map`, `reduce`, `scan`, `filter` to GPU kernels.
      ```
      def render_frame prm w h ... : []u32 =
        tabulate (w * h) (\i ->
          let (x, y) = (i % w, i / w)
          in pack (pixel prm x y))
      ```
      Records compile to registers: a `vec3` is three floats.
    ],
    [
      #align(center, canvas(14, 9, {
        node(3.5, 0.2, 7, 1.4, [Futhark source], col: accent)
        arrow((7, 1.6), (3.5, 3.0)); arrow((7, 1.6), (10.5, 3.0))
        node(0.2, 3.0, 6.6, 2.0, text(size: 13pt)[`futhark webgpu`\ WGSL shaders\ + a wasm host])
        node(7.2, 3.0, 6.6, 2.0, text(size: 13pt)[`futhark multicore`\ a CPU program], col: dim)
        arrow((3.5, 5.0), (3.5, 6.4))
        arrow((10.5, 5.0), (10.5, 6.4), col: dim)
        node(0.2, 6.4, 6.6, 2.0, text(size: 13pt)[the browser,\ 60 fps])
        node(7.2, 6.4, 6.6, 2.0, text(size: 13pt)[byte-exact test renders,\ these slides' images], col: dim)
      }))
      #cap[One source, two targets: refactors are checked to change no byte.]
    ])
]

#slide[From GPU to screen, with no CPU round trip][
  #align(center, canvas(28, 7.2, {
    node(0.2, 0.5, 5.2, 1.6, [TypeScript\ state, input], col: dim)
    arrow((5.4, 1.3), (7.2, 1.3))
    node(7.2, 0.5, 5.8, 1.6, text(size: 12pt)[records and arrays:\ params, drive, spells])
    arrow((13, 1.3), (14.8, 1.3))
